<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (Operator Process Improvements)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E ( Before, there was barely any desk space to assemble motors and no defined test cycle, so swapping motors in and out was slow and clunky. I mapped the workflow with a flow chart, wrote an SOP, and reorganized the space around it. Custom jigs now handle assembling the dovetails on the motors, and each motor has a set place so it's clear where it goes when it's boxed for shipping. The result is a clean, repeatable testing process. Overall cycle time dropped 10 min to 4 min from all of my improvements.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (Overview & Design)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E ( A soldering machine we ordered from a Chinese supplier ran over a 70% scrap rate on MR30 wire harnesses, far too high for production. I built our own machine around the parts that worked and cut scrap to 10-20% at one harness every 12 seconds. I reverse-engineered the original tip-down mechanism and used Onshape parametric part studios to design fixtures that reuse its hardware and align the connector. An X-Y optics table lets us fine-tune the assembly to absorb part tolerances and misalignment down to 0.01mm. Two pneumatic actuators run it, one for the iron tip and one for the solder needles. I had a working prototype in a weekend.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E (Controls & My Role)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 199 0 R
/E ( The machine runs off an ESP32 Cheap Yellow Display board, where the operator sets the soldering profile: temperature, dwell, needle timing, and extrusion length. I did all the mechanical design and built the fixtures. Another person handled most of the software, but I set up SSH and made some changes myself, including better calibration and flashing stall protection and CoolStep onto the stepper driver board.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
